<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD polkit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
"http://www.freedesktop.org/software/polkit/policyconfig-1.dtd">

<!--
  One authentication per burst of usbdiag probes, instead of one per probe.

  Without this file, `pkexec usbdiag probe …` falls under polkit's own
  org.freedesktop.policykit.exec, whose implicit authorization is `auth_admin`
  — no caching, so every single probe asks for a password. Diagnosing a cable
  means running several in a row, and a password prompt per run is how people
  learn to stop reading prompts.

  WHAT ONE AUTHENTICATION BUYS, exactly. Measured by listing polkit's temporary
  authorizations (`pkcheck`, flag `list-temp`) on polkit 124, not assumed: the
  first draft of this comment claimed "the lifetime of the session", and that is
  not what polkit does. Note the flag is spelled without its leading dashes
  above, because an XML comment may not contain two of them in a row — which is
  how this file stopped parsing the first time.

    * only `/usr/local/bin/usbdiag`, and only its `probe` subcommand — that is
      what the two annotations below pin down. `usbdiag labels`, or any other
      binary, is not covered and falls back to polkit's default action.
    * only for the **calling process**. The temporary authorization's subject is
      `unix-process:<pid>` — the window that ran pkexec, not the session. A
      terminal, or a second copy of the app, authenticates on its own, and
      restarting the app throws it away.
    * for **five minutes**, which is polkitd's retention and none of our
      business. `auth_admin_keep` does not mean "until logout".
    * the same audience as before: an administrator. This file changes the
      number of prompts, not who may answer them.

  So what it actually buys is a burst: several probes from one window while a
  cable is being swapped need one authentication rather than one each. That is
  the case worth having, and it is smaller than "one prompt per session".

  The most invasive thing it therefore grants is `probe reenumerate`, which
  takes one device off the bus and puts it back. That probe refuses outright on
  a disk holding a mounted filesystem or a subtree containing an input device,
  and those refusals are not consent-overridable — see crates/usb-probe/src/probe.rs.

  allow_any and allow_inactive deliberately match what the default action
  already allowed. Setting them to `no` would be tighter, but shipping this
  file would then *remove* a capability that existed before it: a `pkexec` over
  ssh, which polkit's own action permits at `auth_admin`, would start being
  refused outright. Making something stop working is not the job of a file
  whose only purpose is to ask fewer times.

  Installed by scripts/install-system.sh. If usbdiag lives anywhere other than
  /usr/local/bin — a distribution package in /usr/bin, say — change exec.path
  to match, or the action will not be found and every probe will prompt again.
  `escalate::tests::the_shipped_policy_covers_a_path_the_finder_would_use`
  keeps this file and that finder in step.
-->

<policyconfig>

  <vendor>usb-analyzer</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/iboalali/USB-Analyzer</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>com.iboalali.usbdiag</icon_name>

  <action id="com.iboalali.usbdiag.probe">
    <description>Run a USB diagnostic probe</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to measure this machine's USB hardware</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/local/bin/usbdiag</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.argv1">probe</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
